between songs. The sermon section includes fields for the sermon title, passage, and any key points the
worship plan should reinforce. The response song field allows the user to choose a song that aligns with the
message and encourages reflection or commitment. The module includes fields for pastoral prayer , oﬀering,
announcements, communion or baptism if applicable, and the closing benediction. A theological safety check
field allows the user to indicate any doctrinal concerns or notes. Finally, a post‑service reflection field gives
space for evaluating engagement, flow, and improvements for future services. These input fields ensure that
every worship plan is intentional, cohesive, pastorally meaningful, and aligned with the theme and scripture.
Worship Planning Output Structure
Worship Planning Output Structure: The Worship Planning module produces a complete, clearly organized
worship plan that follows a consistent structure every time. The output begins with the service overview,
including the service date, sermon theme, primary scripture passage, pastor or worship leader , and selected
service style. The next section presents the call to worship, written as a short pastoral invitation that aligns with
the theme. The opening song or hymn is listed with its title, arrangement type, and a brief rationale explaining
why it fits the theme. The scripture reading section includes the passage reference and an optional note on how
it connects to the service. The worship set section lists each song in order , including title, arrangement,
instrumentation notes, vocal assignments, and a short explanation of how each song reinforces the message.
Transitions are written as brief spoken moments that connect songs to the theme. The sermon section includes
the sermon title, scripture passage, and a summary of the key message the worship plan supports. The
response song is listed with a rationale explaining how it encourages reflection, surrender , or celebration. The
pastoral prayer , oﬀering, announcements, and any special elements such as communion or baptism are
included with simple, clear descriptions. The closing benediction is written as a short pastoral blessing that
aligns with the theme. A theological safety check section summarizes the doctrinal review, confirming that all
songs and elements are biblically accurate and Christ‑centered. The output concludes with a post‑service
reflection section summarizing engagement, flow, and any notes for future improvement. This structure
ensures every worship plan is cohesive, predictable, pastorally meaningful, and aligned with the theme and
scripture.
Worship Planning UI Layout
Worship Planning UI Layout: The Worship Planning screen is designed as a clean, pastoral, modern interface
that guides pastors and worship leaders through the planning process with clarity and ease. The top section
displays the service date, sermon theme, scripture passage, and selected service style in a simple header that
sets the context for the entire plan. Below the header , the left side of the screen contains the input panel,
organized into clearly labeled sections for call to worship, opening song or hymn, scripture reading, worship set
songs, sermon details, response song, prayer moments, oﬀering, announcements, communion or baptism, and
closing benediction. Each input field uses large, readable text boxes with soft edges, warm neutral
backgrounds, and subtle shadows to maintain the pastoral aesthetic. Song selection fields include search and
tag options for theme, scripture, and arrangement type. Instrumentation and transition notes appear as
optional expandable fields beneath each song. A theological safety indicator sits at the bottom of the input
panel, showing a simple green, yellow, or red status based on doctrinal alignment. The right side of the screen
displays the live output preview, updating in real time as the user enters information. The preview uses soft
slate blue backgrounds, warm neutral cards, and clear typography to mirror the final formatted worship plan.
At the bottom of the screen, two primary action buttons allow the user to save the plan or copy it to the
clipboard. The layout is spacious, calm, and intuitive, helping pastors build worship services with confidence,
clarity, and pastoral ease.
Worship Planning AI Workflow
Worship Planning AI Workflow: The Worship Planning module processes user inputs through a structured,
multi‑step workflow that ensures every worship plan is cohesive, pastorally meaningful, and doctrinally safe.
The workflow begins by collecting all input fields, including service date, sermon theme, scripture passage,
service style, songs, transitions, instrumentation notes, sermon details, and special elements. The AI first
validates the inputs to ensure required fields are present and the selected service style matches available
presets. Next, the AI analyzes the sermon theme and scripture passage to establish the theological anchor for
the service. It then evaluates each selected song for thematic alignment, lyrical clarity, and doctrinal accuracy,
applying the theological safety check to flag any concerns. The AI organizes the service flow according to the
standardized output structure, generating a pastoral call to worship, formatting the opening song rationale,
inserting the scripture reading, arranging the worship set with transitions, summarizing the sermon focus, and
selecting or formatting the response song. Instrumentation notes, vocal assignments, and spoken transitions
are integrated into the flow in a natural, pastoral tone. The AI then formats pastoral prayer , oﬀering,
announcements, communion or baptism elements, and the closing benediction. A final theological review is
applied to ensure the entire plan is Christ‑centered, biblically accurate, and consistent with the theme. The AI
produces a clean, structured worship plan in the output preview panel and prepares it for saving or copying.
This workflow ensures every generated worship plan is intentional, cohesive, and aligned with the pastor’s
direction.
Worship Planning Presets
Worship Planning Presets: The Worship Planning module includes a set of predefined service styles that shape
the tone, song selection, instrumentation, transitions, and overall worship flow. The Contemporary preset
emphasizes modern worship songs with a focus on Christ‑centered lyrics, emotional engagement, and a warm,
pastoral tone. It uses acoustic and electric guitars, piano, pads, light percussion, and conversational transitions
that connect each element to the theme. The Traditional preset centers on hymns, reverent liturgical flow, and
formal transitions. It uses piano, organ, and a small vocal ensemble, with hymn selections chosen for
theological richness and congregational familiarity. The Blended preset combines hymns and modern worship
songs in a balanced flow that bridges traditional and contemporary expressions. It uses piano, acoustic guitar ,
light percussion, and simple pastoral transitions that maintain continuity between styles. The Youth preset
emphasizes energetic, identity‑focused worship with modern songs that resonate with students. It uses
acoustic and electric guitars, cajon or light drums, and informal, encouraging transitions that connect scripture
to everyday student life. The Acoustic preset focuses on simplicity, reflection, and intimacy, using acoustic
guitar , piano, and minimal vocals. It features gentle transitions, slower pacing, and songs arranged in
stripped‑down formats that support quiet, contemplative worship. Each preset automatically adjusts tone,
transitions, instrumentation notes, and song rationale to match the selected style, ensuring that every
generated worship plan feels authentic, cohesive, and pastorally appropriate.
Worship Planning Song Database Structure
Worship Planning Song Database Structure: The Worship Planning module uses a structured song database
that stores all relevant information needed for theme alignment, scripture connection, theological safety, and
arrangement selection. Each song entry includes the song title, artist or source, arrangement type such as
contemporary, traditional, acoustic, youth, or blended, and a set of theme tags that describe the song’s focus
areas like surrender , trust, identity, hope, or abiding. Scripture tags link the song to specific passages or biblical
themes, allowing the AI to match songs to sermon texts. Each entry includes a theological notes field
summarizing doctrinal strengths, potential concerns, or clarifications to support the theological safety check.
The database stores lyrical clarity indicators to help identify songs with ambiguous or metaphor-heavy
language. Instrumentation notes describe typical arrangements such as acoustic guitar , piano, pads, organ, or
full band. A transitions field provides recommended spoken moments or pastoral connections that naturally
link the song to worship flow. The database includes congregational engagement indicators describing whether
the song is widely known, easy to sing, or better suited for special music. Additional fields include tempo, mood,
and service style compatibility. Each song entry also contains a usage history field showing how often the song
has been selected and in what contexts. This structure ensures the AI can intelligently select, evaluate, and
recommend songs that are thematically aligned, doctrinally safe, and pastorally appropriate for any worship
plan.
Worship Planning Export Options
Worship Planning Export Options: The Worship Planning module provides simple, streamlined export options
that allow pastors and worship leaders to save, share, and use their worship plans with ease. The primary
export option is Copy to Clipboard, which instantly formats the worship plan into a clean, single‑paragraph
structure that can be pasted into ClickUp, email, planning documents, or church management systems. The
Save to Account option stores the worship plan inside the user’s profile, allowing them to revisit, edit, or
duplicate past services. Each saved plan includes metadata such as date, theme, scripture, service style, and
song selections for easy retrieval. The module also includes a Share Link option that generates a private,
view‑only link for team members such as pastors, worship leaders, musicians, or production volunteers. A
Print‑Friendly View option formats the worship plan into a simple, clean layout suitable for Sunday morning
binders or worship team rehearsals. For MVP, advanced exports such as PDF, Word, or presentation formats are
intentionally excluded to keep the workflow simple and fast. The export system ensures that worship plans are
easy to distribute, easy to reuse, and always presented in a clean, pastoral format that supports clarity and
collaboration.
